select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"What is the evaporator-side COP of a VAR chiller?","back":"COP = Qe / Qin, where Qe = m × Cp × (t_in - t_out) / 3600 (kW) for the chilled water. Qin = m_st × (h_st - h_cond) / 3600 for steam, or m_f × GCV / 3600 for direct fuel firing.","explain":"Qe is the refrigeration effect in kW from the chilled water flow, specific heat and temperature difference. Qin is the thermal input in kW from the steam or fuel rate. Both must be in kW to give a dimensionless COP.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p173","id":"p4-b09-028","paper":4,"order":4401},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"How is the refrigeration effect of a VAR chiller found from the condenser side?","back":"From an overall heat balance: heat rejected at the condenser Qc = refrigeration effect Qe + thermal input Qin, so Qe = Qc - Qin. Qc = Mc × Cp × (t_wo - t_wi) / 3600 (kW) for the cooling water.","explain":"This method needs no separate chilled water measurement, so it gives an independent check on the evaporator-side result. The condenser-side COP is then Qe / Qin.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p174","id":"p4-b09-029","paper":4,"order":4402},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"What is the formula for excess air from the measured O₂ in flue gas?","back":"Excess air EA (%) = O₂ / (21 - O₂) × 100, where O₂ is the measured flue gas oxygen in % (v/v). Actual air supplied = (1 + EA/100) × theoretical air.","explain":"The flue gas O₂ shows air left over after combustion. The 21 is the oxygen percentage of ambient air. The actual air supplied is then the theoretical air scaled by (1 + EA/100).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p176","id":"p4-b09-030","paper":4,"order":4403},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"What is the formula for theoretical air needed for combustion, from the fuel analysis?","back":"Theoretical air (kg/kg fuel) = [11.6 × C + 34.8 × (H₂ - O₂/8) + 4.35 × S] / 100, where C, H₂, O₂ and S are the % carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and sulphur (w/w) in the fuel.","explain":"This uses the fuel analysis, not the flue gas. The oxygen in the fuel is subtracted from the hydrogen term (O₂/8), since it already supplies part of the hydrogen's oxygen needs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p176","id":"p4-b09-031","paper":4,"order":4404},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"In the indirect method of combustion efficiency, which losses are counted, and what is the formula?","back":"Only the dry flue gas loss and the wet flue gas loss are counted. Combustion efficiency = 100 - total flue gas losses (%). All other losses are treated as insignificant and ignored.","explain":"The indirect method estimates efficiency from flue gas analysis rather than measuring heat output directly. Total flue gas loss = dry gas loss + wet gas loss. The result is an estimate because the minor losses are excluded.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p177","id":"p4-b09-032","paper":4,"order":4405},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"What is the formula for the dry flue gas loss (DGL) in the indirect method?","back":"DGL (%) = m_dg × Cp × (T_f - T_a) / GCV × 100, where m_dg is the mass of dry flue gas per kg of fuel, Cp is its specific heat in kcal/kg °C, T_f is the flue gas temperature and T_a the ambient air temperature.","explain":"The dry gas loss is the sensible heat carried up the stack by the dry flue gas, expressed as a share of the fuel's gross calorific value. The dry gas mass is derived from the actual air supplied plus the fuel.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p177","id":"p4-b09-033","paper":4,"order":4406},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"What is the formula for the wet flue gas loss (WGL) in the indirect method?","back":"WGL (%) = (9 × H₂ + M) × (584 + 0.45 × (T_f - T_a)) / GCV × 100, where 584 is the latent heat (kcal/kg) at the partial pressure of water vapour and 0.45 is the specific heat of water vapour (kcal/kg °C).","explain":"The wet gas loss covers the energy in water vapour formed from the fuel's hydrogen (9 × H₂) and its moisture (M). Total flue gas loss = DGL + WGL.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p177","id":"p4-b09-034","paper":4,"order":4407},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"Method 1 of the VAR test: chilled water flow 620 m³/h (density 1000 kg/m³, Cp 4.18 kJ/kg K), inlet 8.8 °C and outlet 6.8 °C. Steam use 3443 kg/h at enthalpy 2734 kJ/kg; condensate enthalpy 418 kJ/kg. Find the refrigeration effect in TR, COP and specific energy consumption.","back":"Qe = about 5.18 million kJ/h = 410 TR; Qin = about 7.97 million kJ/h; COP = 0.65; SEC = about 19,449 kJ/TR (about 4,645 kcal/TR).","explain":"Evaporator-side estimate from the book's sample VAR calculation. The book attributes the deviation from design to the chiller running at less than 50% load.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p178","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the refrigeration effect in kJ/h.","answer":"620 × 1000 × 4.18 × (8.8 - 6.8) = 5,183,200 kJ/h.","explain":"Mass flow in kg/h times Cp times the temperature difference gives kJ/h."},{"prompt":"Convert to TR.","answer":"5,183,200 / (3.516 × 3600) = 410 TR.","explain":"1 TR = 3.516 kW = 12,657.6 kJ/h."},{"prompt":"Find the thermal energy input.","answer":"3443 × (2734 - 418) = 7,974,332 kJ/h.","explain":"Steam flow times the enthalpy drop from steam to condensate."},{"prompt":"Calculate COP.","answer":"5,183,200 / 7,974,332 = 0.65.","explain":"Refrigeration effect divided by thermal input."},{"prompt":"Calculate specific energy consumption.","answer":"3443 × 2316 / 410 = 19,449 kJ/TR, about 4,645 kcal/TR.","explain":"Thermal input per TR of refrigeration; 4.187 kJ per kcal converts units."}],"id":"p4-b09-035","paper":4,"order":4408},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"Method 2 of the VAR test (condenser side): cooling water flow 954 m³/h (density 1000 kg/m³, Cp 4.18), inlet 26.6 °C and outlet 29.9 °C. Using thermal input Qin = 7,974,332 kJ/h from Method 1, find the heat rejected at the condenser, the refrigeration effect in TR and the COP.","back":"Heat rejected Qc = about 13.16 million kJ/h; Qe = Qc - Qin = about 5.19 million kJ/h = about 410 TR; COP = 0.65.","explain":"The condenser-side result should agree with Method 1, which is the check on the test.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p178","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the heat rejected at the condenser.","answer":"954 × 1000 × 4.18 × (29.9 - 26.6) = about 13,159,476 kJ/h.","explain":"Cooling water mass flow times Cp times the temperature rise."},{"prompt":"Find the refrigeration effect.","answer":"Qe = Qc - Qin = 13,159,476 - 7,974,332 = 5,185,144 kJ/h.","explain":"Heat balance across the VAR system."},{"prompt":"Convert to TR.","answer":"5,185,144 / (3.516 × 3600) = 410 TR.","explain":"Same conversion as Method 1."},{"prompt":"Calculate COP.","answer":"5,185,144 / 7,974,332 = 0.65.","explain":"Matches the evaporator-side value."}],"id":"p4-b09-036","paper":4,"order":4409},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"The book's VAR test gives COP 0.65, well below design. What cause does the book suggest?","back":"Operation of the chiller at less than 50% load.","explain":"The book says the deviation from design values may be due to the chiller running below half load. The load level should therefore be checked before concluding the machine is faulty.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p178","id":"p4-b09-037","paper":4,"order":4410},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"A food processing plant's cold air flow is 20,000 m³/h at 1.2 kg/m³, with enthalpy 105 kJ/kg in and 80 kJ/kg out. Its VCR system has COP 3.75 and motor efficiency 90%. It runs 8,000 h/yr at Rs 6/kWh. A VAR system costs Rs 20 lakh; a waste heat boiler costs Rs 6 lakh. Steam use is 5.5 kg per TR; the existing boiler has evaporation ratio 14; FO costs Rs 32,000/tonne. Which option should be recommended?","back":"Option 2 (steam from the waste heat boiler). Option 1 is rejected because steam costs about Rs 598/h against an electricity saving of Rs 296/h. Option 2 payback = 26 / 23.7 = about 1.09 years.","explain":"Solved example from the chapter. The book takes the full base-case VCR energy cost as the annual saving, which implies the waste heat steam is treated as free.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p180","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the base-case refrigeration load.","answer":"(20,000 × 1.2 × (105 - 80)) / (3024 × 4.187) = about 47.4 TR.","explain":"Heat removed in kJ/h divided by kJ per TR-hour."},{"prompt":"Find the base-case motor input power.","answer":"Compressor input = 47.38 × 3024 / (3.75 × 860) = 44.43 kW; motor input = 44.43 / 0.9 = 49.37 kW.","explain":"COP converts refrigeration to electrical input; 860 kcal/h = 1 kW; 0.9 is motor efficiency."},{"prompt":"Find the base-case annual cost.","answer":"49.37 kW × 8000 h = 3.95 lakh kWh; × Rs 6 = Rs 23.7 lakh/yr.","explain":"This is the cost the VAR option must beat."},{"prompt":"Check Option 1 (steam from the existing FO boiler).","answer":"Steam = 5.5 × 47.38 = 261 kg/h; steam cost = Rs 32,000 / 14 per tonne = Rs 2.29/kg, so 261 × 2.29 = Rs 598/h. Electricity saving = 49.37 × 6 = Rs 296/h.","explain":"Steam cost exceeds the saving, so Option 1 is not feasible."},{"prompt":"Check Option 2 (steam from the waste heat boiler) and select.","answer":"Investment = 20 + 6 = Rs 26 lakh; payback = 26 / 23.7 = 1.09 years. Select Option 2.","explain":"Shortest payback of the two options."}],"id":"p4-b09-038","paper":4,"order":4411},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"If the dry-bulb and wet-bulb temperatures of air are both 35 °C, what is its relative humidity?","back":"100%. When dry-bulb and wet-bulb temperatures are equal, the air is saturated.","explain":"The gap between dry-bulb and wet-bulb temperatures shows how far the air is from saturation. A zero gap means the air cannot take up more moisture.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p182","id":"p4-b09-039","paper":4,"order":4412},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"Why is the enthalpy of air used for the heat load calculation, rather than its temperature?","back":"Enthalpy is the total heat content of the air, which depends on both its temperature and its moisture content. It captures both sensible and latent heat; temperature alone does not.","explain":"Two air streams can have the same dry-bulb temperature but carry different moisture and heat. Enthalpy, read from the psychrometric chart using dry and wet bulb temperatures, gives the true heat content for the load.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p182","id":"p4-b09-040","paper":4,"order":4413},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"HVAC Performance Evaluation","front":"A chiller's compressor uses 2 kW per TR. What are its COP and EER (W/W)?","back":"EER = 1.76; COP = 1.76 (the two are numerically the same).","explain":"End-of-chapter question S-5, answered from the book's definitions. With consistent units COP and EER are numerically equal.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 09 p182","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert 1 TR to watts.","answer":"1 TR = 3.516 kW = 3516 W.","explain":"Standard TR equivalent from the chapter."},{"prompt":"Calculate EER.","answer":"EER = 3516 W / 2000 W = 1.76.","explain":"EER is cooling watts divided by input watts."},{"prompt":"Calculate COP.","answer":"COP = 3.516 kW / 2 kW = 1.76.","explain":"Same number as EER because the units match."}],"id":"p4-b09-041","paper":4,"order":4414},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"Which financial appraisal techniques does Chapter 10 cover?","back":"Simple payback, return on investment (ROI), net present value (NPV), internal rate of return (IRR) and sensitivity analysis.","explain":"These are the tools an energy auditor uses to show management the likely return on an energy project. The chapter works through payback, NPV, IRR and profitability index in detail.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p185","id":"p4-b10-001","paper":4,"order":4415},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the difference between variable and fixed costs, with an example of each from the chapter?","back":"Variable costs vary directly with output, for example fuel. Fixed costs do not vary with output, for example the capital cost and annual maintenance of a diesel generator.","explain":"Fixed and variable costs together make up the total operating cost of a plant or project. Example 10.1 classifies each item in the generator's cost as fixed or variable.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p187","id":"p4-b10-002","paper":4,"order":4416},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What happens to the average unit cost of production as output rises, when fixed costs stay the same?","back":"It decreases, because the fixed costs are spread over more units of output.","explain":"In the generator example, fixed costs fall from about 21.5% to 16.37% of total cost when the average output rises from 50 kW to 70 kW. The book attributes this to better use of the capital.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p188","id":"p4-b10-003","paper":4,"order":4417},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the break-even equation for an energy project compared with buying utility power?","back":"UC_util × W_av × n = FC + (UC_prod × W_av × n), where UC_util and UC_prod are the unit costs (Rs/kWh) of utility and self-produced energy, FC is the fixed cost (Rs), W_av is the average power (kW) and n is the hours of operation. Solve for n.","explain":"Break-even is the point where the cost of buying the energy equals the fixed cost plus the cost of producing it. Solving for n gives the operating hours needed to recover the fixed cost.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p188","id":"p4-b10-004","paper":4,"order":4418},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"Why does a higher average output shorten the break-even time of a generator?","back":"Because the capital investment is better utilised, so the fixed costs are recovered over fewer operating hours.","explain":"In Example 10.2 the break-even time falls from 21,000 hours at 50 kW to 15,000 hours at 70 kW for the same generator.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p188","id":"p4-b10-005","paper":4,"order":4419},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the total repayment value (TRV) of a loan under simple interest?","back":"TRV = LV + (IR/100 × LV × P), where LV is the loan value (Rs), IR is the interest rate (%) and P is the repayment period (years).","explain":"Simple interest is a fixed percentage of the borrowed capital, applied to each year of the loan. Lenders usually prefer compound interest, which gives them more over the same period.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p189","id":"p4-b10-006","paper":4,"order":4420},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the total repayment value of a loan under compound interest, and why do lenders usually use it?","back":"TRV = LV × (1 + IR/100)^P. Interest is charged each period on the outstanding loan, which includes unpaid interest from earlier periods. Lenders usually charge compound interest.","explain":"Because unpaid interest is added to the balance, interest accrues on interest. Example 10.3 shows Rs 30,00,000 at 10% over 5 years costs Rs 48,31,530 with compound interest, against Rs 45,00,000 with simple interest.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p189","id":"p4-b10-007","paper":4,"order":4421},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the simple payback period, and what is its formula?","back":"The time for the running total of net savings (before depreciation) to equal the capital cost. PB (years) = CC / AS, where CC is the capital cost (Rs) and AS is the annual net cost saving (Rs) after all operating costs are met.","explain":"The shorter the payback period, the more attractive the project. The chapter notes the maximum acceptable payback varies with the business culture of the organisation.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p190","id":"p4-b10-008","paper":4,"order":4422},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What are the two main weaknesses of the simple payback method?","back":"(1) It ignores savings that accrue after the payback period has ended. (2) It ignores the time value of money: invested money should earn interest, so Rs 1000 today is worth more than Rs 1000 in 10 years.","explain":"These weaknesses are the reason the chapter moves on to discounted cash flow methods, namely net present value and internal rate of return.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p191","id":"p4-b10-009","paper":4,"order":4423},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"How are discounting and compounding related?","back":"They are opposite processes. Compounding determines the future value of present cash flows; discounting determines the present value of future cash flows.","explain":"Discounting uses an assumed discount rate, usually an interest rate, to convert each future cash flow into its value today.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p191","id":"p4-b10-010","paper":4,"order":4424},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is net present value (NPV), and how are costs and savings signed in the calculation?","back":"The sum of the present values of all yearly cash flows over the life of the project, with costs as negative values and savings as positive values. The higher the NPV, the more attractive the project.","explain":"Each cash flow is multiplied by its discount factor to give its present value (PV = S × DF). The NPV method then adds these values together, including the initial capital cost.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p192","id":"p4-b10-011","paper":4,"order":4425},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What are the formulas for future value and present value?","back":"Future value: FV = D × (1 + IR/100)^n, where D is the deposit. Present value: PV = S × (1 + IR/100)^(-n), where S is the cash flow in n years. Equivalently, PV = S × DF, with DF = 1 / (1 + IR/100)^n.","explain":"The minus sign in the exponent of the present value formula is lost in the OCR but is confirmed by the chapter's discount factor of 0.926 for 8% in year 1 (1/1.08 = 0.926).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p192","id":"p4-b10-012","paper":4,"order":4426},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"Why should the discount rate for an NPV analysis be set slightly above the interest rate at which the capital is borrowed?","back":"It makes the analysis slightly pessimistic, which guards against the uncertainty in predicting future savings and interest rates.","explain":"The credibility of the NPV method depends on a realistic prediction of future interest rates, which are often unpredictable. A slightly higher discount rate prudently lowers the NPV.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p194","id":"p4-b10-013","paper":4,"order":4427},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the internal rate of return (IRR)?","back":"The discount rate at which the net present value of a project becomes zero. The higher the IRR, the more attractive the project.","explain":"IRR is found by trying discount rates until the NPV changes sign, then interpolating between the two rates. Example 10.7 gives an IRR of 12.93%.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p194","id":"p4-b10-014","paper":4,"order":4428},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"How does NPV differ from IRR as an appraisal tool?","back":"NPV is essentially a comparison tool that ranks several projects. IRR is designed to assess whether a single project will achieve a target rate of return.","explain":"The two methods look similar and often agree, but they answer different questions. Use NPV to choose between projects, and IRR to test a single project against a target.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p196","id":"p4-b10-015","paper":4,"order":4429},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"How is the IRR found by linear interpolation between two discount rates?","back":"IRR = r1 + (r2 - r1) × NPV1 / (NPV1 - NPV2), where NPV1 is positive at discount rate r1 and NPV2 is negative at discount rate r2.","explain":"This assumes NPV changes in a straight line between the two rates, so the result is an approximation. Example 10.7 applies it with r1 = 12% (NPV +459.5) and r2 = 16% (NPV -1508.5) to get 12.93%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p196","id":"p4-b10-016","paper":4,"order":4430},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What is the profitability index (PI), and how is it used?","back":"PI = sum of the discounted net savings / capital cost. The higher the profitability index, the more attractive the project.","explain":"The chapter applies it to the NPV values of Example 10.6 (Rs 10,254 and Rs 10,867.80 against a capital cost of Rs 30,000), giving 0.342 and 0.362. Project 2 is therefore the better proposal.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p196","id":"p4-b10-017","paper":4,"order":4431},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"Which factors does the basic payback and NPV analysis not allow for?","back":"(1) Depreciation: the capital value of plant and equipment falls over time. (2) General inflation: it reduces the value of savings as time passes. (3) Salvage value of the equipment at the end of the analysis period.","explain":"Examples 10.5 and 10.6 illustrate the basic principles but leave these out. Examples 10.9 and 10.10 show how salvage value and inflation change the NPV.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p196","id":"p4-b10-018","paper":4,"order":4432},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"Does ignoring the salvage value of equipment understate or overstate the NPV?","back":"It understates it. In Example 10.9 the NPV is Rs 4,489.50 with a salvage value of Rs 1,500, but only Rs 3,468 without it.","explain":"Salvage value is cash received at the end of the analysis period, so it adds a positive term to the final year's cash flow.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p197","id":"p4-b10-019","paper":4,"order":4433},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"How does inflation change the NPV of a project, according to the chapter's example?","back":"It reduces it. In Example 10.10, with 5% inflation and the discount rate kept at 8%, the NPV falls from Rs 4,489.50 to Rs 4,397.88.","explain":"The book says this is to be expected, because general inflation erodes the value of future profits. Inflation is handled by converting cash flows to real values before discounting at the real interest rate.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p198","id":"p4-b10-020","paper":4,"order":4434},
{"kind":"fact","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"What are the real value and inflation factor formulas, and what is the real interest rate?","back":"Real value RV = S × IF, where the inflation factor IF = (1 + R/100)^(-n), R is the inflation rate (%) and n is the number of years. Real interest rate = discount rate - inflation rate.","explain":"The minus sign in the exponent is lost in the OCR but is confirmed by the inflation factor of 0.952 for 5% in year 1 (1/1.05 = 0.952). Real value therefore falls with time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p198","id":"p4-b10-021","paper":4,"order":4435},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"The capital cost of a diesel generator is Rs 9,00,000. It produces 219 MWh a year, maintenance is Rs 30,000 a year, and fuel costs Rs 3.50/kWh. Over 5 years, find the total cost and the share of fixed costs.","back":"Total cost = Rs 48,82,500. Fixed costs (capital plus maintenance) = Rs 10,50,000, which is 21.5% of the total.","explain":"Example 10.1 of the chapter. At an average output of 70 kW instead of 50 kW the fuel cost would be Rs 53,65,500 and the fixed-cost share would fall to about 16.4%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p187","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the fixed capital cost.","answer":"Capital cost of generator = Rs 9,00,000 (fixed).","explain":"Capital is a fixed cost because it does not change with output."},{"prompt":"Find the fixed maintenance cost.","answer":"Rs 30,000 × 5 years = Rs 1,50,000 (fixed).","explain":"Maintenance is treated as fixed in the book's table."},{"prompt":"Find the variable fuel cost.","answer":"219,000 kWh × Rs 3.50 × 5 years = Rs 38,32,500 (variable).","explain":"Fuel cost varies directly with the energy produced."},{"prompt":"Find the total cost and the fixed share.","answer":"Total = 9,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 38,32,500 = Rs 48,82,500. Fixed share = 10,50,000 / 48,82,500 = 21.5%.","explain":"Fixed costs are the capital plus maintenance."}],"id":"p4-b10-022","paper":4,"order":4436},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"A generator's fixed costs are Rs 10,50,000 (capital plus five years of maintenance). Utility power costs Rs 4.5/kWh and the generator's fuel costs Rs 3.50/kWh. Find the break-even operating hours at an average output of 50 kW, and then at 70 kW.","back":"At 50 kW the break-even is 21,000 hours. At 70 kW it is 15,000 hours.","explain":"Example 10.2 of the chapter. Higher average output reduces break-even time because the capital is better utilised.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p188","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the break-even equation.","answer":"UC_util × W_av × n = FC + UC_prod × W_av × n.","explain":"Cost of buying energy equals fixed cost plus cost of producing it."},{"prompt":"Solve at 50 kW.","answer":"4.5 × 50 × n = 10,50,000 + 3.5 × 50 × n, so 225n - 175n = 10,50,000 and n = 21,000 hours.","explain":"Collect the n terms on one side."},{"prompt":"Solve at 70 kW.","answer":"4.5 × 70 × n = 10,50,000 + 3.5 × 70 × n, so 315n - 245n = 10,50,000 and n = 15,000 hours.","explain":"Same method with the higher output."},{"prompt":"Interpret the result.","answer":"Break-even falls from 21,000 h to 15,000 h.","explain":"The fixed cost is recovered over fewer hours when output rises."}],"id":"p4-b10-023","paper":4,"order":4437},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"A company borrows Rs 30,00,000 at 10% a year for 5 years. Find the total repayment and the monthly repayment under (i) simple interest and (ii) compound interest.","back":"Simple: total Rs 45,00,000, monthly Rs 75,000. Compound: total Rs 48,31,530, monthly about Rs 80,525.","explain":"Example 10.3 of the chapter. The compound method costs the borrower Rs 3,31,530 more than the simple method.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p189","steps":[{"prompt":"Simple interest total.","answer":"30,00,000 + (10/100 × 30,00,000 × 5) = Rs 45,00,000.","explain":"Interest is a fixed percentage of the loan for each year."},{"prompt":"Simple interest monthly.","answer":"45,00,000 / (5 × 12) = Rs 75,000.","explain":"Spread over 60 months."},{"prompt":"Compound interest by year.","answer":"Year 1: 33,00,000; year 2: 36,30,000; year 3: 39,93,000; year 4: 43,92,300; year 5: 48,31,530.","explain":"Each year's interest is charged on the outstanding balance, including earlier interest."},{"prompt":"Compound interest total and monthly.","answer":"Total = 30,00,000 × (1.10)^5 = Rs 48,31,530; monthly = 48,31,530 / 60 = Rs 80,525.","explain":"The closed-form formula gives the same total as the year-by-year figures."}],"id":"p4-b10-024","paper":4,"order":4438},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"A cogeneration installation reduces a company's annual energy bill by Rs 4,86,000. Its capital cost is Rs 22,20,000 and annual maintenance and operating costs are Rs 42,000. Find the simple payback period.","back":"Simple payback = 5.0 years.","explain":"Example 10.4 of the chapter. The book's text names both a cogeneration plant and a boiler installation for the same figures.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p191","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the net annual saving.","answer":"4,86,000 - 42,000 = Rs 4,44,000.","explain":"Net saving is after operating and maintenance costs."},{"prompt":"Find the payback period.","answer":"PB = 22,20,000 / 4,44,000 = 5.0 years.","explain":"Capital cost divided by annual net saving."},{"prompt":"Check the meaning of the result.","answer":"Rs 4,44,000 a year over 5 years recovers the Rs 22,20,000 capital.","explain":"Payback is the time at which the running total of net savings equals the capital cost."}],"id":"p4-b10-025","paper":4,"order":4439},
{"kind":"problem","topic":"Financial Analysis","front":"Rs 22,20,000 is invested at 8% a year. What is its value after 5 years?","back":"FV = Rs 32,61,908 (interest earned Rs 10,41,908).","explain":"Example 10.5 of the chapter. Conversely, Rs 32,61,908 in 5 years is worth Rs 22,20,000 today at 8%. The chapter's text shows 22,00,000 once, which is a typo.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 4 Ch 10 p192","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the future value formula.","answer":"FV = D × (1 + IR/100)^n.","explain":"Compounding adds interest to the deposit each year."},{"prompt":"Calculate the growth factor.","answer":"(1.08)^5 = 1.46933.","explain":"Five years of 8% growth."},{"prompt":"Calculate the future value.","answer":"22,20,000 × 1.46933 = Rs 32,61,908.","explain":"Matches the book's Rs 32,61,908.40 to rounding."},{"prompt":"Find the interest earned.","answer":"32,61,908 - 22,20,000 = Rs 10,41,908.","explain":"The difference is the interest over five years."}],"id":"p4-b10-026","paper":4,"order":4440}
]$cards$::jsonb);
